# 🟡 Level 2 – Network Troubleshooting

## DNS Lookup

Convert domain names into IP addresses.

```bash
nslookup google.com
```

or

```bash
dig google.com
```

---

## Trace Packet Route

```bash
traceroute google.com
```

Shows the path packets take across networks.

---

## View Listening Ports

```bash
ss -tuln
```

This displays:

* TCP ports
* UDP ports
* Listening services

---

## View Active Connections

```bash
ss -tunap
```

Useful during incident investigations.

---

# SSH (Secure Shell)

SSH allows encrypted remote administration.

Example:

```bash
ssh user@192.168.1.20
```

Unlike Telnet, SSH encrypts communication.

---

## SSH Authentication

Two common methods:

### Password Authentication

Simple but weaker if passwords are weak.

---

### SSH Key Authentication

More secure.

Components:

```text
Private Key
        │
Authentication
        │
Public Key
```

The private key stays secret; the public key is placed on the server.

---

# SSH Hardening

✔ Disable root login.

✔ Prefer SSH keys over passwords.

✔ Change default settings only when appropriate and documented.

✔ Limit login attempts.

✔ Use MFA where supported.

✔ Keep OpenSSH updated.

✔ Restrict users who may log in.

